#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110294 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/id #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17212" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$(5s + 4)x = 9x$\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s$?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = bx$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b$, then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx$. In this case,\n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5s &= 5 \\\\\ns &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110297 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" :question/math-academy-id "e-18852" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12$\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r$?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c$, and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x$, and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x$, we have\n$k + 4 = 6$.\nSolving this equation for $k$, we get\n$k|= 2$.\n- Equating the constants, we have\n$3r = 12$.\nSolving this equation for $r$, we get\n$r|= 4$.\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4$, we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110298 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18854" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n1. $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n2. $-3x = -4x + 6$\n3. $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n$4x + 1|= 4x + 1; 4x + 1 - 4x|= 4x + 1 - 4x; 1|= 1$.\nThis equation is true for all values of $x$. Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n- For the second equation, we have\n$-3x|=-4x + 6; - 3x + 4x|=-4x + 6 + 4x; x|= 6$.\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6$.\n- For the third equation, we have\n$2x + 7|= 2x - 1; 2x + 7 - 2x|= 2x - 1 - 2x; 7|=-1$.\nThis equation is false for all values of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95e44dcf-460e-54d3-a79a-545445d92869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108471} {:answer/id #uuid "9618c04e-6bf8-5f69-a644-698a27923a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108472} {:answer/id #uuid "1d24c4ff-5342-57d1-8ece-085b802a55e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108473} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee4e4a1-22dc-53d4-b3c8-52726c8dcfd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108474} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccd84a9-85ae-5ac8-9811-356f69f9e30c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95e44dcf-460e-54d3-a79a-545445d92869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108471} :answer-field/id #uuid "236405db-5656-5faa-b11d-7c347c65c5ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108470}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71c3d793-6b55-5392-ac94-1149b022497f" :question/math-academy-id "q-287966" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n1. $2x = x + 1$\n2. $2x + 1 = 2x + 1$\n3. $2x + 1 = 2x + 3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =x+1-x \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x=x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+1-2x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x+1=2x+1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+3 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+3-2x \\\\ 1 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c86e3f84-9e2f-5a4c-8749-316046db82c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108477} {:answer/id #uuid "0899a853-f0f3-5d95-be59-7cb8a48cf339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108478} {:answer/id #uuid "609de082-704e-5d74-ad4b-200857c13b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108479} {:answer/id #uuid "7382da56-8e7c-53d6-8f78-e5a7b7826d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108480} {:answer/id #uuid "7655143b-a4d2-5ea5-a29b-c1ff24ec4972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7382da56-8e7c-53d6-8f78-e5a7b7826d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108480} :answer-field/id #uuid "c294e8e9-667b-5f1d-b5cb-cca55f68f46b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108476}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "573bc8f1-012d-5d7e-834a-c6ec17bb0b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-287970" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$2px = 3x$\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p$?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e90fb5-7ccc-55ff-a8ba-43b72fa068bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108483} {:answer/id #uuid "07837fa5-7c0a-5227-9fd9-6d452dcc1dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108484} {:answer/id #uuid "0940e311-a515-5a9e-81c6-d30522603c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108485} {:answer/id #uuid "14abe6eb-7590-5eb5-8f55-dd0fd4fefbdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108486} {:answer/id #uuid "50b506cf-aa8d-5d1b-93d9-9b8f7eb28293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07837fa5-7c0a-5227-9fd9-6d452dcc1dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108484} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd4965a-6d29-54b7-98ea-60ec18ab2900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108482}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "435ae769-5577-5924-a2b3-9f23f7e0ccc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-287971" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$(p - 3)x = 4x$\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p$?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=p-3,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\np-3=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c63cda8-2840-56f2-8271-c21f55da3cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c63cda8-2840-56f2-8271-c21f55da3cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252086} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1df2cf-9566-5004-8ba6-32d65599da18" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252085}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceb241e1-ab0e-5069-a882-fcb4fcc9530e" :question/math-academy-id "q-287975" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p-3,\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p-3=5.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p & =8 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "953fa2ba-af1c-5d5b-92de-347ec77a1e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108496} {:answer/id #uuid "9f330b74-ad10-53b2-a364-1de04a0fa52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108497} {:answer/id #uuid "a8278bd5-8321-5c87-980f-5501df6b2934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108498} {:answer/id #uuid "a99bcd7a-8959-5a08-a84f-4251bfd6ed4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108499} {:answer/id #uuid "056b8daa-e8f4-5e31-8c80-4995aac93253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "056b8daa-e8f4-5e31-8c80-4995aac93253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108500} :answer-field/id #uuid "c94fa0bd-028a-5d48-93f5-e4a31a10c005" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108495}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b24019f-f592-514c-8874-109e97014d8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-288419" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n1. $3x = 2x + 5$\n2. $4x - 2 = 4x - 2$\n3. $5x + 3 = 5x + 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =2x+5 \\\\ 3x-2x & =2x+5-2x \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x=2x+5$ has the unique solution $x=5.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2 & =4x-2 \\\\ 4x-2-4x & =4x-2-4x \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x-2=4x-2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & =5x+1 \\\\ 5x+3-5x & =5x+1-5x \\\\ 3 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb7c1e6-18fb-5883-8ac3-e8038cf665d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252089} {:answer/id #uuid "135c8097-040c-5eaa-bd48-8c1bd1be1ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252090} {:answer/id #uuid "57cfaaeb-39d3-5e02-8d43-1b5d2094213d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252091} {:answer/id #uuid "2a307c03-89b0-56b3-b26c-60c731cd0cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252092} {:answer/id #uuid "0041155f-65d3-516a-bbc3-ce5282877436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57cfaaeb-39d3-5e02-8d43-1b5d2094213d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252091} :answer-field/id #uuid "564067cd-fa2c-5738-b6b8-fba47708cbc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252088}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7142601-1b63-5b13-9c8c-802df3891cac" :question/math-academy-id "q-288425" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $-3x+2=-3x+7$\n- $6x+1=6x+5$\n- $7x-4=6x-6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2 & =-3x+7 \\\\ -3x+2+3x & =-3x+7+3x \\\\ 2 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+5 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+5-6x \\\\ 1 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4 & =6x-6 \\\\ 7x-4-6x & =6x-6-6x \\\\ x-4 & =-6 \\\\ x-4+4 & =-6+4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $7x-4=6x-6$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5702e069-3db0-5b29-b6ad-3fe304c18fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252096} {:answer/id #uuid "a447939d-baec-57fb-98de-5900bfe1090c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252097} {:answer/id #uuid "a27f1b00-e14a-567a-a8ff-6dd69fe87a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252098} {:answer/id #uuid "53979b98-5b48-58b3-acd8-d72d22989ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252099} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc0db71-42fd-5933-ab48-9d72613dfc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27f1b00-e14a-567a-a8ff-6dd69fe87a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252098} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d9ab049-7072-55db-a7a3-b956c894bb34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252095}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4aab251b-8c37-5d06-8cf8-88c1adaf045f" :question/math-academy-id "q-288427" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $2x-5=x-2$\n- $6x+1=6x+9$\n- $-4x+3=-4x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ 2x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ x-5 & =-2 \\\\ x-5+5 & =-2+5 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=x-2$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+9-6x \\\\ 1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3 & =-4x+3 \\\\ -4x+3+4x & =-4x+3+4x \\\\ 3 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4x+3=-4x+3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d4f19a8-615b-503a-b052-ad2574729d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252103} {:answer/id #uuid "aac22344-b395-557c-90b1-3becc9f31e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252104} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5cbd91-cd0b-558f-8b5b-bef6ded474dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252105} {:answer/id #uuid "b7877191-cd2a-570c-9ef3-9bb1a3d886d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252106} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe15aed-1947-5e4f-994b-30705109cb1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c5cbd91-cd0b-558f-8b5b-bef6ded474dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252105} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e8989c5-075e-503e-b8ef-f75bec0f669c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252102}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b71e5d0-a8bd-5f07-b336-d4214ef97d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-288436" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n5px=10x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=5p,\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p=10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4827fd78-c6ad-5a7a-9bf9-15f0165b1a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252110} {:answer/id #uuid "12931b0c-eac3-5909-ba7b-172b10fd03e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252111} {:answer/id #uuid "04cd0694-9be7-5d1b-abc9-84c983507215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252112} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b8ce83-4463-590d-b26c-44b7c9326b20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252113} {:answer/id #uuid "8163c938-d3ff-5c31-8e6c-20c03591a83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8163c938-d3ff-5c31-8e6c-20c03591a83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252114} :answer-field/id #uuid "067f3197-ce56-5f72-9b4e-f65c161b0f14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252109}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7707541-1581-52e4-89a8-380771ef493c" :question/math-academy-id "q-288440" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n-4kx=8x\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-4k,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n-4k=8.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f9d736a-c682-5a3b-834a-cf8f4b6bcfea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f9d736a-c682-5a3b-834a-cf8f4b6bcfea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252117} :answer-field/id #uuid "e095caa4-cf23-54b1-b6e5-42a31cc98a69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252116}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a4d57ff-6bcc-52bd-8911-d563e2cabe8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-288583" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ and $m$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t+m?$\n\n$t+m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $t$ and $m$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n1-m=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $t=3$ and $m=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\nt+m=3+(-3)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c01c0cec-8be7-558a-b294-c591d8a541d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c01c0cec-8be7-558a-b294-c591d8a541d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252120} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cf47335-2ab5-5b4b-a694-689490ef6c09" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252119}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "458632c2-a121-5956-b70c-1083bf4954df" :question/math-academy-id "q-288584" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+3=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=2.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=2$ and $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=2+3=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "269a5f6f-e47b-5c97-80e5-197b32aa5f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252123} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f1183d-d642-5afb-a2bc-8afbed9821c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252124} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d43ed6-0aef-5730-8742-3a4e494d4158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252125} {:answer/id #uuid "750c2253-4e2c-5ed0-a2ce-8cc8141a1176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252126} {:answer/id #uuid "963db8ec-57d3-5788-9a6d-6551c3bcd118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6d43ed6-0aef-5730-8742-3a4e494d4158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252125} :answer-field/id #uuid "76c46080-f181-5de9-aa39-b8ee838c7452" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252122}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81c20257-9b1e-55db-b3ce-0210f947d920" :question/math-academy-id "q-288591" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $3x+2=4x+1$\n- $2x-5=3x-3$\n- $5x-4=5x-4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =4x+1 \\\\ 3x+2-3x & =4x+1-3x \\\\ 2 & =x+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+2=4x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =3x-3 \\\\ 2x-5-2x & =3x-3-2x \\\\ -5 & =x-3 \\\\ -5+3 & =x-3+3 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=3x-3$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-4 & =5x-4 \\\\ 5x-4-5x & =5x-4-5x \\\\ -4 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-4=5x-4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4497ef92-56d2-5e6c-8447-e67ad0a2a6dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252130} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3159c6-3502-5e5b-b14a-190e83c2ad5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252131} {:answer/id #uuid "02150bc2-83b1-5cc9-836e-726a68f7422d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252132} {:answer/id #uuid "5e13c442-1e5d-55b3-a899-50ce6ae315ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252133} {:answer/id #uuid "24804afb-c846-547d-b85e-18b379f9fa84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a3159c6-3502-5e5b-b14a-190e83c2ad5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252131} :answer-field/id #uuid "fad43b24-e43d-59fd-8531-592734d1c035" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252129}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "107e60f6-2335-50ce-b0c8-2a4b9c9873ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-288622" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+2=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n5n=15.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=5$ and $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=5+3=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86ba9a51-3dcb-57d4-a109-8860b0b0ffba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252137} {:answer/id #uuid "46dcdda3-7271-593e-85cc-255bbbc6fe1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252138} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb62a81-7e10-51d1-983d-1fb6361f5d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252139} {:answer/id #uuid "54211d78-72db-5c19-a472-1cfab206980d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252140} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0060fb-5925-59c5-912c-22a664f97716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46dcdda3-7271-593e-85cc-255bbbc6fe1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252138} :answer-field/id #uuid "54e67142-eba3-5d1c-bb60-d1124dacdbc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252136}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb84cb7f-b888-57b2-be16-cb2f94243f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-288623" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2kx+r=9x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2kx+r=9x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2k=9.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\nr=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{9}{2}$ and $r=6,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+r=\\frac{9}{2}+6=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b39409-8889-5493-b425-a39858693269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252144} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb56e57-0792-5a8f-be57-957098d787fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252145} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fc743b-33e4-5360-af1c-36857ae316fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252146} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8428fb-81b8-5ac9-90df-fffde296b0d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252147} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7f8a64-2f69-5092-993e-a304984f7948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fb56e57-0792-5a8f-be57-957098d787fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252145} :answer-field/id #uuid "3113796c-a9b9-5cca-82d8-e74e254c5944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252143}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35d716e9-efe6-5b38-accd-60b85b179fe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-288627" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $p$ and $q$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\nq=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $p=8$ and $q=20,$ we have\n\n$$\np+q=8+20=28.\n$$"}]}
